ot {x}_{1}(t)$\n- $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$\n- $x(t)=-3\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+5\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$" :question/worked-solution "The *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system, then for any constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ the linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis also a solution of the system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expressions.\n\n- Option I might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ Notice that the multiplier is not a constant.\n\n- Option II might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ Notice that the multiplier of the second term is not a constant.\n\n- Option III is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n-3\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+5\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ with constant multiplies ${c}_{1}=-3$ and ${c}_{2}=5,$ respectively.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-330312/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "95de2c3e-5810-5aa0-a1d0-b98b012572d4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-330312"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-321646/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "da1cbd6a-d100-525d-8c47-d5e0a04f9664" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-321646/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "afbcbe14-05f3-5ce2-b005-307783d3d854" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-321646/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "17a83b23-35d4-5f08-ae81-58326f12cd9b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-321646/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" :answer/id #uuid "6259ed36-0c07-5329-a1bc-bc38bd7017da" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-321646/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "685a0f61-5846-5fdc-a9f1-2b2551abb6bb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-321646/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "211127a3-7555-5b9b-9d49-60482c6b0cda" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-321646/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer-field/choices ["q-321646/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-321646/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-321646/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-321646/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" "q-321646/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-321646" :question/id #uuid "1fe51e55-362f-5884-8ef8-5903c807d0bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-321646" :question/problem "Suppose ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are both solutions of the homogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same system?\n\n- $x(t)=2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$\n- $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)-t\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$\n- $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)⊙{x}_{2}(t)$ (element-wise product)" :question/worked-solution "The *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system, then for any constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ the linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis also a solution of the system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expressions.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+(-1)\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ with constant multiplies ${c}_{1}=2$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ respectively.\n\n- Option II might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ Notice that the multiplier of the second term is not a constant.\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-321646/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "95de2c3e-5810-5aa0-a1d0-b98b012572d4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-321646"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-330348"] :question/problem "Suppose ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are both solutions of the homogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same system?\n\n- $x(t)={e}^{t}\\,{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)$\n- $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)$\n- $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)\\times {x}_{2}(t)$ (cross-product)" :question/worked-solution "The *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system, then for any constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ the linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis also a solution of the system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expressions.\n\n- Option I might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ Notice that the multiplier of the first term is not a constant.\n\n- Option II is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ with constant multiplies ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=1,$ respectively.\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-330353/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "de49b246-fb4c-5045-b331-530e89a82d4d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-330353/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" :answer/id #uuid "b0e9bd83-1db9-5dae-b35b-35e148e9e343" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-330353/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "652be7cd-4bec-54d4-bd02-b2e57ce97148" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-330353/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "1c12237a-0c79-5810-a2b1-140e5cbb712f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-330353/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "c3d0308f-7f2b-5da3-a9b6-f0de0e676b5a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-330353/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c1fbd11e-1a82-52c0-8f3c-b20bd4913f95" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-330353/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer-field/choices ["q-330353/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-330353/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" "q-330353/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-330353/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-330353/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-330353" :question/id #uuid "ca8f9356-4beb-53da-a0cd-65d91466d466" :question/math-academy-id "q-330353" :question/problem "Suppose ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are both solutions of the homogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same system?\n\n- $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$\n- $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$\n- $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)⊙{x}_{2}(t)$ (element-wise product)" :question/worked-solution "The *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system, then for any constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ the linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis also a solution of the system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expressions.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a linear combination of the solutions with constant multiplies ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ respectively.\n\n- Option II might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ Notice that the multiplier of the second term is not a constant.\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-330353/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "95de2c3e-5810-5aa0-a1d0-b98b012572d4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-330353"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-76598/selection/4d535c6086c7a9f95bd70851375c08ef53c63bba83b748a6c2c1affc0d5a1928" :answer/id #uuid "825fc261-844d-5eda-94c7-7a1f677d8044" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}-8{e}^{-t} \\\\ 7{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76598/selection/177b00c25bd15bbe305c905375a276934a9f16b40c7eb28e606e5fc7195ef3f0" :answer/id #uuid "70f3b7b9-9d5d-57de-ae3d-70084ee0e08d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}7{e}^{t} \\\\ -8{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76598/selection/be05d469c334d97d986ecfb170aaedd16a239a22a781ec70aef0ebdda1dec945" :answer/id #uuid "b99e9499-1236-503d-b7f9-157360d9db5e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}8{e}^{-t} \\\\ -7{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76598/selection/52013df5197508670c6861a36186a1e6d47e39f84fdd48f9ce94170c4f3222fc" :answer/id #uuid "a9eb3b1c-674d-5d07-83d1-793841f4f923" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}-8{e}^{t} \\\\ 7{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76598/selection/7e31c0fb37c16e1e238498db560a1976e010915346a44137373d8171d2050349" :answer/id #uuid "89a7cebc-3ca3-50c3-860e-a67e73c7e1cc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}-7{e}^{-t} \\\\ 8{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76598/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "628aea4e-2075-510c-b134-0c6495d1f43a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-76598/selection/4d535c6086c7a9f95bd70851375c08ef53c63bba83b748a6c2c1affc0d5a1928" :answer-field/choices ["q-76598/selection/4d535c6086c7a9f95bd70851375c08ef53c63bba83b748a6c2c1affc0d5a1928" "q-76598/selection/177b00c25bd15bbe305c905375a276934a9f16b40c7eb28e606e5fc7195ef3f0" "q-76598/selection/be05d469c334d97d986ecfb170aaedd16a239a22a781ec70aef0ebdda1dec945" "q-76598/selection/52013df5197508670c6861a36186a1e6d47e39f84fdd48f9ce94170c4f3222fc" "q-76598/selection/7e31c0fb37c16e1e238498db560a1976e010915346a44137373d8171d2050349"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-76598" :question/id #uuid "7660a51c-5da6-5b8b-a119-8b11e908a01d" :question/math-academy-id "q-76598" :question/problem "If $\\,u(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,v(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]\\,$ are solutions to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$, find $Aw(t)$ where $w(t)=[\\begin{aligned}8{e}^{-t} \\\\ 7{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Since $u(t)$ and $v(t)$ are solutions to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ the superposition principle guarantees that \n\n$$\ncu(t)+dv(t)\n$$\n\n is also a solution to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for all $c,d\\in R.$\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\nw(t)=8u(t)+7v(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $w$ is a solution to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t).$ So, in order to find $Aw(t)$ we just need to find ${w}^{′}(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Aw & ={w}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(8{e}^{-t})^{′} \\\\ (7{e}^{t})^{′}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8{e}^{-t} \\\\ 7{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-76598/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "318f129c-066d-50bd-934a-90722ba4fc93"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-76598"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-76600/