{2}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because it lacks the particular solution term ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that $3\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $3$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-330308/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f6920b1-2a94-5a47-840f-c823c9f70e36"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-330308"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-330290"] :question/problem "Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)+f(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n\n- $3\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$\n- ${x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)+2\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$\n- $-4\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${x}_{h}(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ then ${x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t),…,{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)+⋯+{c}_{k}{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith ${c}_{1},{c}_{2},…,{c}_{k}\\in R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=3\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n{{3\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\n- Option II might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that ${x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)+2\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $2$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\n- Option III is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=-4\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n{{-4\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-330298/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "1121b58e-ffe1-5b21-94fc-04cf3b8000ae" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-330298/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "96c61a3d-66f4-5706-957b-f4bf49bff2a3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-330298/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "88df457a-dd56-52e2-8b21-2679acf71996" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-330298/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "e0cc2535-a6ef-52a0-8674-85514eef50df" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-330298/selection/b1a9f2bb9f9bd59b66c906c9ab98446a26ec623744a7f92356bb121785f6cea5" :answer/id #uuid "4048de4a-7d9f-56c6-be50-cfcd28776e55" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-330298/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "7185c857-c246-515f-8e24-0fe682f37939" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-330298/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer-field/choices ["q-330298/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-330298/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-330298/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-330298/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-330298/selection/b1a9f2bb9f9bd59b66c906c9ab98446a26ec623744a7f92356bb121785f6cea5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-330298" :question/id #uuid "9feea13f-3341-5519-8dd7-7a36f01f2708" :question/math-academy-id "q-330298" :question/problem "Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)+f(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n\n- $-2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+4\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$\n- ${x}_{2}(t)-{x}_{1}(t)+2\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$\n- $-7\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${x}_{h}(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ then ${x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t),…,{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)+⋯+{c}_{k}{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith ${c}_{1},{c}_{2},…,{c}_{k}\\in R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option I might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that $-2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+4\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $4$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\n- Option II might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that ${x}_{2}(t)-{x}_{1}(t)+2\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $2$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\n- Option III is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=-7\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n{{-7\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-330298/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f6920b1-2a94-5a47-840f-c823c9f70e36"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-330298"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21110"] :question/problem "Suppose $\\mathbf{x}_1(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2(t)$ are both solutions of the homogeneous system\n$\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\ \\mathbf{x}(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same system?\n\n- $\\mathbf{x}(t) = 6 \\cdot \\mathbf{x}_1(t) - \\mathbf{x}_2(t)$\n\n- $\\mathbf{x}(t) = -3 \\cdot \\mathbf{x}_1(t) + 4 \\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)$\n\n- $\\mathbf{x}(t) = (1+t^2)\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_1(t) + e^{t}\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)$" :question/worked-solution "The *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if $\\mathbf{x}_{1}(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system, then for any constants $c_1$ and $c_2,$ the linear combination \n\n$$\nc_{1}\\mathbf{x}_{1}(t)+c_{2}\\mathbf{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis also a solution of the system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expressions.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n6 \\cdot \\mathbf{x}_1(t) + (-1) \\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)\n$$\n\nis a linear combination of the solutions $\\mathbf{x}_{1}(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_{2}(t)$ with constant multiplies $c_1=6$ and $c_2=-1,$ respectively.\n\n- Option II is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n-3 \\cdot \\mathbf{x}_1(t) + 4 \\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)\n$$\n\nis a linear combination of the solutions $\\mathbf{x}_{1}(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_{2}(t)$ with constant multiplies $c_1=-3$ and $c_2=4,$ respectively.\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions $\\mathbf{x}_{1}(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_{2}(t).$ Notice that the multipliers of the first and the second terms are not constant.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21112"] :question/problem "If ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/assets/32e9cba3d7ee75019489dcc8a9c82f1b854bfa77af49e22fa9f51d4412cba95d.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/assets/dc64b0c557c838cf31e652ebdb8eee91d1fee8c947b343b16fceaf765c1f8517.png) are solutions to $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t),$ find $A\\mathbf{w}(t)$ where ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066703/assets/64b157c381c05102b51320592e61ffff5c4d611af4bd15679f1f2b38f3d01b10.png)" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{u}(t)$ and $\\mathbf{v}(t)$ are solutions to $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t),$ the superposition principle guarantees that \n\n$$\nc\\mathbf{u}(t) + d\\mathbf{v}(t)\n$$\n\n is also a solution to $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)$ for all $c,d\\in\\mathbb{R}.$\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{w}(t)=4\\mathbf{u}(t)+6\\mathbf{v}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t).$ So, in order to find $A\\mathbf{w}(t)$ we just need to find $\\mathbf{w}'(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Aw & ={w}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(4{e}^{4t})^{′} \\\\ (6{e}^{5t})^{′}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16{e}^{4t} \\\\ 30{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21111"] :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{x}_1(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\ \\mathbf{x}(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n$\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\ \\mathbf{x}(t) + \\mathbf{f}(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n\n- $\\mathbf{x}_1(t) + 2\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t) + \\mathbf{x}_p(t)$\n\n- $4\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t) + \\mathbf{x}_p(t)$\n\n- $-\\mathbf{x}_1(t) - \\mathbf{x}_p(t)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $\\mathbf{x}_h(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t) + \\mathbf{f}(t),$ then $\\mathbf{x}_h(t) + \\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if $\\mathbf{x}_{1}(t),$ $\\mathbf{x}_{2}(t),\\ldots, \\mathbf{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\nc_1 \\mathbf{x}_{1}(t) + c_2\\mathbf{x}_{2}(t) + \\cdots + c_k\\mathbf{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith $c_1, c_2, \\ldots, c_k \\in\\mathbb R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_h(t) = 1\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_1(t) + 2\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\mathbf{x}_1(t) + 2\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)}_{\\mathbf{x}_h(t)} + \\mathbf{x}_p(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t) + \\mathbf{f}(t).$\n\n- Option II is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_h(t) = 4\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{4\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)}_{\\mathbf{x}_h(t)} + \\mathbf{x}_p(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t) + \\mathbf{f}(t).$\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that $-\\mathbf{x}_1(t) - \\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_h(t) + \\mathbf{x}_p(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}_h(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $-1$ next to the particular solution $\\mathbf{x}_p(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
